{"reason": "The initial plan's three changes (stacking, feature compression, and hyperparameter tuning) directly target the core issue of high-dimensional noisy features causing overfitting (CV log loss ~0.41). Historical experiments confirm that DeBERTa-v3-large as a frozen feature extractor combined with classical features and XGBoost is a solid base, but the feature dimensionality (4544) is too high relative to 17k samples. Change #2 (feature compression) is the most impactful as it reduces noise and enables deeper trees (Change #3) to learn meaningful patterns. The two-stage stacking (Change #1) further mitigates fold-wise variance. All three changes are synergistic and should be implemented across the feature engineering, model design, and training modules.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add PCA on DeBERTa embeddings (reduce from 1024 to 256 components) and SelectKBest on combined features (keep top 3000). Implement PCA using sklearn's PCA with n_components=256, fit on training embeddings only, then transform validation and test. For SelectKBest, use chi2 scoring and keep top 3000 features from the combined (PCA-reduced embeddings + handcrafted features) matrix. This reduces noise and dimensionality, enabling the model to focus on discriminative patterns.", "model_design": "Implement two-stage stacking: first train 5-fold XGBoost models on the reduced feature space (after PCA and SelectKBest), generating out-of-fold predictions. These OOF predictions (3 probabilities per fold) are concatenated with the original reduced features to form a new training set for a meta-learner (second XGBoost with same hyperparameters). The meta-learner learns to blend the fold-level predictions and original features, reducing overfitting and capturing inter-class boundaries better.", "training_evaluation": "Update XGBoost hyperparameters to: learning_rate=0.02, max_depth=8, subsample=0.7, colsample_bytree=0.6, min_child_weight=3, reg_lambda=3.0, reg_alpha=0.5, num_boost_round=1500 with early_stopping_rounds=100. The lower learning rate with more rounds and deeper trees, combined with stronger regularization, will exploit the reduced feature space and stacking to achieve lower log loss. Preserve the 5-fold StratifiedKFold structure and multi:softprob objective."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's three changes (stacking, feature compression, and hyperparameter tuning) directly target the core issue of high-dimensional noisy features causing overfitting (CV log loss ~0.41). Historical experiments confirm that DeBERTa-v3-large as a frozen feature extractor combined with classical features and XGBoost is a solid base, but the feature dimensionality (4544) is too high relative to 17k samples. Change #2 (feature compression) is the most impactful as it reduces noise and enables deeper trees (Change #3) to learn meaningful patterns. The two-stage stacking (Change #1) further mitigates fold-wise variance. All three changes are synergistic and should be implemented across the feature engineering, model design, and training modules.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add PCA on DeBERTa embeddings (reduce from 1024 to 256 components) and SelectKBest on combined features (keep top 3000). Implement PCA using sklearn's PCA with n_components=256, fit on training embeddings only, then transform validation and test. For SelectKBest, use chi2 scoring and keep top 3000 features from the combined (PCA-reduced embeddings + handcrafted features) matrix. This reduces noise and dimensionality, enabling the model to focus on discriminative patterns.",
    "model_design": "Implement two-stage stacking: first train 5-fold XGBoost models on the reduced feature space (after PCA and SelectKBest), generating out-of-fold predictions. These OOF predictions (3 probabilities per fold) are concatenated with the original reduced features to form a new training set for a meta-learner (second XGBoost with same hyperparameters). The meta-learner learns to blend the fold-level predictions and original features, reducing overfitting and capturing inter-class boundaries better.",
    "training_evaluation": "Update XGBoost hyperparameters to: learning_rate=0.02, max_depth=8, subsample=0.7, colsample_bytree=0.6, min_child_weight=3, reg_lambda=3.0, reg_alpha=0.5, num_boost_round=1500 with early_stopping_rounds=100. The lower learning rate with more rounds and deeper trees, combined with stronger regularization, will exploit the reduced feature space and stacking to achieve lower log loss. Preserve the 5-fold StratifiedKFold structure and multi:softprob objective."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan identified overfitting as the root cause, evidenced by training loss dropping to near zero while validation loss increased sharply after epoch 2. Two modifications were proposed: label smoothing and gradient clipping adjustment. Both are training strategy changes, so the 'training_evaluation' module is the most appropriate. Similar historical experiments show that using gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0) alone was insufficient to prevent overfitting, but label smoothing combined with tighter clipping (max_norm=0.5) can regularize large pretrained models on small datasets. The current execution confirms severe overfitting and extreme prediction probabilities (e.g., 1e-6), validating the need for these changes. Data processing and model architecture are adequate and should remain unchanged.", "module": ["training_evaluation"], "plan": {"training_evaluation": "Add label smoothing with epsilon=0.1 to the CrossEntropyLoss by replacing the criterion with `nn.CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=0.1)`. This modifies the target distribution to penalize overconfidence, addressing the overfitting where training loss approaches zero. Also reduce gradient clipping max_norm from 1.0 to 0.5 to stabilize updates, as gradients become noisy after rapid overfitting. Implement these changes in the training loop by replacing the criterion and adjusting the `clip_grad_norm_` call. Keep all other hyperparameters and logic unchanged to maintain reproducibility."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identified overfitting as the root cause, evidenced by training loss dropping to near zero while validation loss increased sharply after epoch 2. Two modifications were proposed: label smoothing and gradient clipping adjustment. Both are training strategy changes, so the 'training_evaluation' module is the most appropriate. Similar historical experiments show that using gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0) alone was insufficient to prevent overfitting, but label smoothing combined with tighter clipping (max_norm=0.5) can regularize large pretrained models on small datasets. The current execution confirms severe overfitting and extreme prediction probabilities (e.g., 1e-6), validating the need for these changes. Data processing and model architecture are adequate and should remain unchanged.",
  "module": ["training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "training_evaluation": "Add label smoothing with epsilon=0.1 to the CrossEntropyLoss by replacing the criterion with `nn.CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=0.1)`. This modifies the target distribution to penalize overconfidence, addressing the overfitting where training loss approaches zero. Also reduce gradient clipping max_norm from 1.0 to 0.5 to stabilize updates, as gradients become noisy after rapid overfitting. Implement these changes in the training loop by replacing the criterion and adjusting the `clip_grad_norm_` call. Keep all other hyperparameters and logic unchanged to maintain reproducibility."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan identified overfitting as the root cause (train loss ~0.0005 vs val log loss 0.504) and proposed data augmentation and two-stage LR schedule. Historical experiments show that gradient clipping + label smoothing alone (failed experiment) was insufficient; synonym replacement augmentation (successful experiment DeBERTa+XGBoost used tf-idf char n-grams as feature augmentation) combined with warmup+cosine decay (successful experiment ModernBERT) can reduce overfitting. The current code already implements label smoothing and clipping, so further improvement requires augmentation and a better scheduler. Data augmentation is best added in data_processing_and_feature_engineering (on-the-fly transforms). The scheduler change belongs to training_evaluation. Model design is adequate.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add on-the-fly synonym replacement augmentation to the AuthorDataset.__getitem__ method. With probability 0.3, randomly select 15% of non-stopword tokens in the text and replace each with a synonym from WordNet (via nltk). If a token has no synonym, keep it unchanged. After augmentation, tokenize as before. This forces the model to focus on semantic structure rather than exact word patterns, reducing overfitting. Implementation: store an nltk wordnet lemmatizer and stopword set, iterate through tokens after simple whitespace splitting, skip stopwords and non-alpha tokens, replace if random.random() < 0.15. Keep all other dataset logic identical (max_length, padding, label handling).", "training_evaluation": "Replace the CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with a linear warmup from 0 to 3e-5 over first 20% of total steps followed by cosine decay to 1e-6 over remaining steps. Increase max epochs to 12, reduce patience to 2. This prevents disruptive LR restarts that cause validation loss spikes (as seen in execution where restart at epoch 5 increased val loss). Update scheduler step call to use `scheduler.step()` per training iteration (not per epoch) for correct step count. Keep early stopping and best model saving logic unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identified overfitting as the root cause (train loss ~0.0005 vs val log loss 0.504) and proposed data augmentation and two-stage LR schedule. Historical experiments show that gradient clipping + label smoothing alone (failed experiment) was insufficient; synonym replacement augmentation (successful experiment DeBERTa+XGBoost used tf-idf char n-grams as feature augmentation) combined with warmup+cosine decay (successful experiment ModernBERT) can reduce overfitting. The current code already implements label smoothing and clipping, so further improvement requires augmentation and a better scheduler. Data augmentation is best added in data_processing_and_feature_engineering (on-the-fly transforms). The scheduler change belongs to training_evaluation. Model design is adequate.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add on-the-fly synonym replacement augmentation to the AuthorDataset.__getitem__ method. With probability 0.3, randomly select 15% of non-stopword tokens in the text and replace each with a synonym from WordNet (via nltk). If a token has no synonym, keep it unchanged. After augmentation, tokenize as before. This forces the model to focus on semantic structure rather than exact word patterns, reducing overfitting. Implementation: store an nltk wordnet lemmatizer and stopword set, iterate through tokens after simple whitespace splitting, skip stopwords and non-alpha tokens, replace if random.random() < 0.15. Keep all other dataset logic identical (max_length, padding, label handling).",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with a linear warmup from 0 to 3e-5 over first 20% of total steps followed by cosine decay to 1e-6 over remaining steps. Increase max epochs to 12, reduce patience to 2. This prevents disruptive LR restarts that cause validation loss spikes (as seen in execution where restart at epoch 5 increased val loss). Update scheduler step call to use `scheduler.step()` per training iteration (not per epoch) for correct step count. Keep early stopping and best model saving logic unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan's hard negative mining approach directly targets the root cause of structured misclassifications, as evidenced by the per-fold log loss variability (0.3978 to 0.4289). The current validation score of 0.4103 indicates room for improvement. Historical successful experiments show that XGBoost with DeBERTa embeddings works well, while failed attempts with label smoothing on ModernBERT overfitted. Hard negative mining is a training strategy change that does not alter the proven feature pipeline or model architecture, making it the proper module to modify. The plan refines the implementation by using the cross-validation split structure to generate fold-specific hard examples and ensemble, based on the historical success of multi-stage training with XGBoost.", "module": ["training_evaluation"], "plan": {"training_evaluation": "Implement two-stage training with hard negative mining. After the initial cross-validation training, collect the fold-wise OOF predictions. For each fold, identify the hard examples (bottom 10-20% by predicted probability for the true class) and assign them double sample weight (weight=2). Retrain a second XGBoost model on the full training data using these instance weights. Finally, ensemble the first and second models by averaging their predicted probabilities. This addresses the structured misclassifications without modifying the feature extraction or model architecture. Preserve all existing code structure: the cross-validation loop, final model retraining, and submission generation should remain unchanged except for adding the weight calculation and second model training."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's hard negative mining approach directly targets the root cause of structured misclassifications, as evidenced by the per-fold log loss variability (0.3978 to 0.4289). The current validation score of 0.4103 indicates room for improvement. Historical successful experiments show that XGBoost with DeBERTa embeddings works well, while failed attempts with label smoothing on ModernBERT overfitted. Hard negative mining is a training strategy change that does not alter the proven feature pipeline or model architecture, making it the proper module to modify. The plan refines the implementation by using the cross-validation split structure to generate fold-specific hard examples and ensemble, based on the historical success of multi-stage training with XGBoost.",
  "module": ["training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "training_evaluation": "Implement two-stage training with hard negative mining. After the initial cross-validation training, collect the fold-wise OOF predictions. For each fold, identify the hard examples (bottom 10-20% by predicted probability for the true class) and assign them double sample weight (weight=2). Retrain a second XGBoost model on the full training data using these instance weights. Finally, ensemble the first and second models by averaging their predicted probabilities. This addresses the structured misclassifications without modifying the feature extraction or model architecture. Preserve all existing code structure: the cross-validation loop, final model retraining, and submission generation should remain unchanged except for adding the weight calculation and second model training."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: training loss drops to 0.0859 while validation log loss plateaus at 0.378, indicating overfitting due to limited representation from the single [CLS] token. The initial plan's proposed multi-scale feature extraction addresses this root cause by leveraging ModernBERT's hierarchical representations. A successful historical experiment used DeBERTa-v3-large as a feature extractor with XGBoost, but that changes the model architecture entirely. Instead, we refine the initial plan by keeping ModernBERT as the backbone and only modifying the classification head (model_design) and data processing (data_processing_and_feature_engineering) to implement hierarchical pooling. This avoids the failed approach of solely adding label smoothing (which showed no improvement) and instead focuses on representation richness.", "module": ["model_design", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the single [CLS] token classification head with a hierarchical multi-scale pooling mechanism. Extract hidden states from ModernBERT layers [12, 16, 20, 24] during forward pass. Apply learned per-layer attention pooling to aggregate token representations into 512-dimensional vectors, concatenate across layers to form 2048-d, then pass through a two-layer MLP (2048→1024→3) with LayerNorm and dropout (0.3). This captures syntactic and stylistic features at multiple linguistic levels, directly addressing the representation bottleneck that caused the plateau.", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Increase max_length from 256 to 512 to capture more contextual information per sample. Implement overlapping chunking for long texts (stride=256) and aggregate predictions via mean pooling. This provides richer stylistic context, which is critical for authorship attribution as shown in successful historical experiments. Keep the author encoding and train/validation split unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's diagnosis: training loss drops to 0.0859 while validation log loss plateaus at 0.378, indicating overfitting due to limited representation from the single [CLS] token. The initial plan's proposed multi-scale feature extraction addresses this root cause by leveraging ModernBERT's hierarchical representations. A successful historical experiment used DeBERTa-v3-large as a feature extractor with XGBoost, but that changes the model architecture entirely. Instead, we refine the initial plan by keeping ModernBERT as the backbone and only modifying the classification head (model_design) and data processing (data_processing_and_feature_engineering) to implement hierarchical pooling. This avoids the failed approach of solely adding label smoothing (which showed no improvement) and instead focuses on representation richness.",
  "module": ["model_design", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the single [CLS] token classification head with a hierarchical multi-scale pooling mechanism. Extract hidden states from ModernBERT layers [12, 16, 20, 24] during forward pass. Apply learned per-layer attention pooling to aggregate token representations into 512-dimensional vectors, concatenate across layers to form 2048-d, then pass through a two-layer MLP (2048→1024→3) with LayerNorm and dropout (0.3). This captures syntactic and stylistic features at multiple linguistic levels, directly addressing the representation bottleneck that caused the plateau.",
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Increase max_length from 256 to 512 to capture more contextual information per sample. Implement overlapping chunking for long texts (stride=256) and aggregate predictions via mean pooling. This provides richer stylistic context, which is critical for authorship attribution as shown in successful historical experiments. Keep the author encoding and train/validation split unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan correctly identifies overfitting as the root cause: training loss drops to near zero while validation loss increases after epoch 2, and submission probabilities are extreme (0.000126, 1.0). The execution results confirm a plateau at 0.3025 validation log loss with severe overconfidence. The initial plan proposed three changes: backbone replacement (smaller model), MixUp augmentation, and Focal Loss with label smoothing. However, a successful historical experiment (DeBERTa-v3-large frozen + XGBoost) demonstrates that feature extraction with non-linear classifiers works well, while a failed experiment using ModernBERT-large with only label smoothing and gradient clipping (max_norm=0.5) still resulted in overfitting (0.3025). Based on this, the backbone replacement is kept to reduce capacity, but instead of training a full transformer, the refined plan will freeze a smaller backbone (deberta-v3-base) and train a lightweight classification head. MixUp is removed due to complexity and potential noise; instead, powerful handcrafted features (stylometric, POS, TF-IDF) are added as input to the classifier to capture author style robustly. Focal Loss and label smoothing are replaced by standard CrossEntropy with class weighting, as the frozen backbone already prevents overfitting. Learning rate is increased for the head. These modules are the most promising because they directly address overfitting with a proven combination from historical experiments.", "module": ["model_design", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace ModernBERT-large with microsoft/deberta-v3-base as a frozen feature extractor. Freeze all backbone layers and train only a new classification head: a dropout layer (p=0.3) followed by a linear layer mapping 768-dim embeddings to 3 classes. Use CrossEntropyLoss with class weights as before, but without label smoothing or Focal Loss. Increase learning rate for the head to 5e-4 with AdamW. This reduces overfitting by limiting trainable parameters to only the head, while leveraging rich pretrained representations. The backbone provides robust features; training only the head prevents memorization of the small dataset.", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "In addition to the frozen DeBERTa embeddings, compute handcrafted features to capture authorial style: (1) stylometric features (punctuation density, uppercase ratio, average sentence length, etc.), (2) POS-style linguistic features (ratios of pronouns, articles, prepositions using spaCy), and (3) TF-IDF char n-grams (n=1-4, max 2000 features). Concatenate all features (768 + handcrafted) to form a combined representation. Use StandardScaler on handcrafted features. This enriches the input with discriminative stylistic signals that are robust to overfitting, as successful historical experiments show combination of transformer embeddings and handcrafted features works well for this task."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan correctly identifies overfitting as the root cause: training loss drops to near zero while validation loss increases after epoch 2, and submission probabilities are extreme (0.000126, 1.0). The execution results confirm a plateau at 0.3025 validation log loss with severe overconfidence. The initial plan proposed three changes: backbone replacement (smaller model), MixUp augmentation, and Focal Loss with label smoothing. However, a successful historical experiment (DeBERTa-v3-large frozen + XGBoost) demonstrates that feature extraction with non-linear classifiers works well, while a failed experiment using ModernBERT-large with only label smoothing and gradient clipping (max_norm=0.5) still resulted in overfitting (0.3025). Based on this, the backbone replacement is kept to reduce capacity, but instead of training a full transformer, the refined plan will freeze a smaller backbone (deberta-v3-base) and train a lightweight classification head. MixUp is removed due to complexity and potential noise; instead, powerful handcrafted features (stylometric, POS, TF-IDF) are added as input to the classifier to capture author style robustly. Focal Loss and label smoothing are replaced by standard CrossEntropy with class weighting, as the frozen backbone already prevents overfitting. Learning rate is increased for the head. These modules are the most promising because they directly address overfitting with a proven combination from historical experiments.",
  "module": ["model_design", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace ModernBERT-large with microsoft/deberta-v3-base as a frozen feature extractor. Freeze all backbone layers and train only a new classification head: a dropout layer (p=0.3) followed by a linear layer mapping 768-dim embeddings to 3 classes. Use CrossEntropyLoss with class weights as before, but without label smoothing or Focal Loss. Increase learning rate for the head to 5e-4 with AdamW. This reduces overfitting by limiting trainable parameters to only the head, while leveraging rich pretrained representations. The backbone provides robust features; training only the head prevents memorization of the small dataset.",
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "In addition to the frozen DeBERTa embeddings, compute handcrafted features to capture authorial style: (1) stylometric features (punctuation density, uppercase ratio, average sentence length, etc.), (2) POS-style linguistic features (ratios of pronouns, articles, prepositions using spaCy), and (3) TF-IDF char n-grams (n=1-4, max 2000 features). Concatenate all features (768 + handcrafted) to form a combined representation. Use StandardScaler on handcrafted features. This enriches the input with discriminative stylistic signals that are robust to overfitting, as successful historical experiments show combination of transformer embeddings and handcrafted features works well for this task."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The evolution trajectory shows progress from 0.3025 (overfitted large model) to 0.6106 (frozen backbone + shallow head) but has plateaued. The current approach freezes the backbone and trains only a linear classifier, which is overly simplistic for this task. The successful pattern from Step 1 (ModernBERT fine-tuning) achieved 0.3025 despite overfitting, indicating that fine-tuning a transformer backbone is powerful. The plateau suggests we need a mid-ground: fine-tune the last few layers of a smaller backbone (distilbert or albert) with strong regularization, rather than freezing entirely or fine-tuning fully. This Tier 2 change (representation & components) should break the plateau by allowing adaptive feature learning while controlling overfitting.", "module": ["model_design"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace frozen DeBERTa-v3-base + linear head with a fine-tuned DistilRoBERTa-base (distilroberta-base) with a multi-layer head (two linear layers with LayerNorm, GELU activation, and dropout 0.4). Use gradual unfreezing: keep backbone frozen for first 3 epochs, then unfreeze top 4 layers. Apply LabelSmoothingCrossEntropy loss (smoothing=0.1) and AdamW with learning rate 3e-5 for backbone (after unfreeze) and 1e-3 for head. Use cosine annealing scheduler with warmup. This approach balances adaptation and overfitting, leveraging historical success of transformer fine-tuning (Step 1) while avoiding full overfitting."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The evolution trajectory shows progress from 0.3025 (overfitted large model) to 0.6106 (frozen backbone + shallow head) but has plateaued. The current approach freezes the backbone and trains only a linear classifier, which is overly simplistic for this task. The successful pattern from Step 1 (ModernBERT fine-tuning) achieved 0.3025 despite overfitting, indicating that fine-tuning a transformer backbone is powerful. The plateau suggests we need a mid-ground: fine-tune the last few layers of a smaller backbone (distilbert or albert) with strong regularization, rather than freezing entirely or fine-tuning fully. This Tier 2 change (representation & components) should break the plateau by allowing adaptive feature learning while controlling overfitting.",
  "module": ["model_design"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace frozen DeBERTa-v3-base + linear head with a fine-tuned DistilRoBERTa-base (distilroberta-base) with a multi-layer head (two linear layers with LayerNorm, GELU activation, and dropout 0.4). Use gradual unfreezing: keep backbone frozen for first 3 epochs, then unfreeze top 4 layers. Apply LabelSmoothingCrossEntropy loss (smoothing=0.1) and AdamW with learning rate 3e-5 for backbone (after unfreeze) and 1e-3 for head. Use cosine annealing scheduler with warmup. This approach balances adaptation and overfitting, leveraging historical success of transformer fine-tuning (Step 1) while avoiding full overfitting."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current frozen ModernBERT + XGBoost pipeline yields high log loss (~0.6775), confirming the initial plan's diagnosis that fine-tuning is needed. Successful historical experiments (fine-tuning ModernBERT with a classification head) achieved significantly lower loss (<0.3). Therefore, the most promising modules to modify are 'model_design' (to adopt the fine-tuning architecture with combined pooling and classification head) and 'training_evaluation' (to replace the XGBoost training loop with end-to-end fine-tuning using weighted cross-entropy, early stopping, and the schedule from the successful experiment). The 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' module requires minimal change—only label encoding and tokenization—so it is not selected.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Change: Replace the frozen ModernBERT + XGBoost pipeline with a ModernBertForSequenceClassification model that uses a pooled representation (CLS token concatenated with mean pooling of all token embeddings) followed by dropout (p=0.1) and a linear classification head with 3 outputs. Use the ModernBERT-large backbone; set all backbone parameters to trainable. Loss: weighted CrossEntropyLoss with class weights inversely proportional to class frequencies. Why: CLS+mean pooling captures both focal and global stylistic features; fine-tuning adapts representations to the author classification task, as demonstrated by successful fine-tuning experiments achieving log loss <0.3. How: Modify the model forward to output hidden states, implement custom pooling, and use the custom head. Interface: preserve model output probabilities with shape (batch, 3).", "training_evaluation": "Change: Replace XGBoost cross-validation and retraining with end-to-end fine-tuning over 5 epochs with AdamW (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01), CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler (T_0=2, T_mult=2), gradient accumulation (steps=2), gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0), mixed precision (AMP), and early stopping (patience=3 on validation log loss). Use stratified 80/15/5? Actually use 5-fold cross-validation? Use a single 85/15 stratified split for efficiency. Why: The successful historical experiment used this training recipe; it directly optimizes the classification loss and avoids the two-stage separation. How: Implement a PyTorch training loop with the described components, evaluate on validation set each epoch, save best model, and generate test predictions with clipped softmax probabilities. Interface: train function receives model, dataloaders, and device; returns trained model and validation scores."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current frozen ModernBERT + XGBoost pipeline yields high log loss (~0.6775), confirming the initial plan's diagnosis that fine-tuning is needed. Successful historical experiments (fine-tuning ModernBERT with a classification head) achieved significantly lower loss (<0.3). Therefore, the most promising modules to modify are 'model_design' (to adopt the fine-tuning architecture with combined pooling and classification head) and 'training_evaluation' (to replace the XGBoost training loop with end-to-end fine-tuning using weighted cross-entropy, early stopping, and the schedule from the successful experiment). The 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' module requires minimal change—only label encoding and tokenization—so it is not selected.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Change: Replace the frozen ModernBERT + XGBoost pipeline with a ModernBertForSequenceClassification model that uses a pooled representation (CLS token concatenated with mean pooling of all token embeddings) followed by dropout (p=0.1) and a linear classification head with 3 outputs. Use the ModernBERT-large backbone; set all backbone parameters to trainable. Loss: weighted CrossEntropyLoss with class weights inversely proportional to class frequencies. Why: CLS+mean pooling captures both focal and global stylistic features; fine-tuning adapts representations to the author classification task, as demonstrated by successful fine-tuning experiments achieving log loss <0.3. How: Modify the model forward to output hidden states, implement custom pooling, and use the custom head. Interface: preserve model output probabilities with shape (batch, 3).",
    "training_evaluation": "Change: Replace XGBoost cross-validation and retraining with end-to-end fine-tuning over 5 epochs with AdamW (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01), CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler (T_0=2, T_mult=2), gradient accumulation (steps=2), gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0), mixed precision (AMP), and early stopping (patience=3 on validation log loss). Use stratified 80/15/5? Actually use 5-fold cross-validation? Use a single 85/15 stratified split for efficiency. Why: The successful historical experiment used this training recipe; it directly optimizes the classification loss and avoids the two-stage separation. How: Implement a PyTorch training loop with the described components, evaluate on validation set each epoch, save best model, and generate test predictions with clipped softmax probabilities. Interface: train function receives model, dataloaders, and device; returns trained model and validation scores."
  }
